//Compile Time polymorphism

Compile-time polymorphism means the compiler decides which function/operator to use at compile time.

The two main examples are:

1. Function Overloading

Same function name, different parameters:

class Calculator {
public:
    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    int add(int a, int b, int c) {
        return a + b + c;
    }
};

The compiler decides which add() to call based on the arguments.

2. Operator Overloading

We define how an operator behaves for our objects:

p3 = p1 + p2;

The compiler determines which overloaded + operator to use.

Simple definition

Compile-time polymorphism = polymorphism where the function/operator to be used is decided by the compiler at compile time.

Main examples:
Function Overloading + Operator Overloading